      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Risk of cardiovascular and metabolic disease is higher in adults who were relatively thin at birth and had subsequent accelerated weight gain. This specific pattern of weight gain may relate to unfavorable cardiometabolic markers already in childhood. We prospectively assessed whether children with different patterns of overweight development from age 3 months to 11 years had distinct levels of cardiometabolic markers at age 12 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS/METHODS",
          "content": "We used data of 1500 children participating in the PIAMA birth cohort that started in 1996/1997. Parents reported height and weight during 10 waves of follow-up from age 3 months to 11 years. Four distinct overweight development patterns were derived using longitudinal latent class analysis; 'never'; 'early transient'; 'gradually developing' and 'persistent' overweight. Cardiometabolic markers (total-to-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (TC/HDLC) ratio, blood pressure (BP), glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c)) were assessed at age 12 years in 1500 children."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Children who developed overweight gradually and children with persistent overweight throughout childhood, at age 12 years had a 2-3-fold higher risk of having high (>90th centile) TC/HDLC ratio, systolic and diastolic BP, compared with children who were never overweight. In children who gradually developed overweight, TC/HDLC ratio was 0.75 higher (95% confidence interval (CI) 0.54-0.96); systolic BP 4.90\u2009mmHg higher (95% CI 2.45-7.36) and diastolic BP 1.78\u2009mmHg higher (95% CI 0.07-3.49) than in children who never had overweight. Estimates for children with persistent overweight were similar."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Children with gradually developing overweight, and those with persistent overweight had unfavorable cholesterol and blood pressure levels already at age 12 years, whereas children with early transient overweight avoided these unfavorable outcomes. Our results support the hypothesis that specific overweight patterns predispose to an adverse cardiometabolic profile, which is already apparent in early adolescence before progressing to adult cardiometabolic disease."
        }
      ]},
